[Music]  there's a lot of controversy over the  origins of the coronavirus that sparked  the pandemic questions over where the  virus came from have fueled another  debate over a kind of research where  scientists tweak the genetic information  of a virus or other pathogen to give it  new properties it's called gain of  function and this isn't the first time  it's come under scrutiny it's used in  research years ago made way for two of  the most controversial studies in modern  science see those titles they don't look  all that concerning but some thought  that details were  for those experiments scientists  modified a potentially deadly virus and  supercharged it virologists were testing  how a type of bird flu which can be  deadly to humans but doesn't transmit  well among people would need to change  genetically to have Pandemic potential  in the 2000s the flu strain involved in  those studies that caused major health  problems globally but still it wasn't as  contagious as some Public Health experts  thought a major pandemic causing virus  could be for the studies the scientists  altered the virus's genetic information  in very specific ways that would help  the pathogens spread among ferrets more  easily ferrets are often used as  standards for humans because they're  susceptible to some of the same viruses  in one study the unmodified version of  the virus didn't spread through the air  among ferrets but the genetically  modified flu had a new capability  Airborne transmission  that's a key ingredient of a pandemic  virus that new ability is what gain of  function refers to the virus gains  capabilities it didn't have before when  reports became public that researchers  had been successful in modifying an  already deadly virus to make it even  more worrisome many fear that gain of  function research gone wrong could  unleash a global pandemic rules were put  in place to better police the research  there was even a moratorium on this kind  of work but that didn't last so what  does all that have to do with this  pandemic this year questions over the  origins of the covet virus went from  Fringe to mainstream people wanted to  know did it accidentally leak from a lab  or did it spill over from nature because  of human animal contact that question is  still under investigation but that  uncertainty is part of what strong  attention back to gain a function there  is some debate about whether some of the  work that research labs in Wuhan were  doing was in fact gain of function  Johannes were the first coveted cases  were recorded so people wondered whether  handling of viruses by Labs there could  have accidentally led to a leak that  sparked the first covet outbreaks the  labs have repeatedly said that's not the  case my co-worker Amy Dr Marcus is one  of the reporters on our health and  science team who has been covering the  origin story and more recently gain of  function research I called her up to ask  her why the gain of function debate felt  so urgent again  when we've looked at this question  before we've talked about the potential  for causing a pandemic in a kind of  almost hypothetical way now we're living  through a pandemic it's very real now in  a way that it wasn't during the previous  rounds of debate and I think that's one  reason why the debate over gain a  function feels so sharp and so immediate  so the research is so controversial why  do researchers do it Amy had some  answers for us there are many scientists  who ardently and convincingly  argue that it's very important to  understand what a pathogen might do next  one of the scientists said to me it's  like knowing your enemy you want to know  how to fight against what happens in the  future and some infectious disease  researchers argue that the only way to  do that is to make genetic tweaks that  help them understand what turns the  virus from run-of-the-mill to pandemic  causing they also say that work helps  with testing drugs and vaccines gain of  function research can involve microbes  or other pathogens that don't initially  pose pandemic threats to us a lot of  work that's done in the gain of function  field involves not human pathogens but  bat viruses but  these can make genetic changes that may  eventually turn into diseases that  affect humans the emergence of a new  dangerous virus that's what scares  people and it's why some gain of  function research especially the kind  that involves risky pathogens is under  the microscope again but gain of  function extends Beyond infectious  disease to other fields there it's not  really as controversial because it  doesn't carry the same public health  risks gain of function research is the  Workhorse of Modern Biology so it's  unlikely it'll go away altogether Amy  told me that researchers are already  doing gain of function work on the  covet-causing virus so we can be better  prepared to tweak our vaccines as new  variants arise  so where do we go from here that gets us  back to where we started the origins  question we still don't know where the  virus came from and we may never know  definitively but getting answers could  help us figure out where to draw the  line on the riskiest of gain of function  research whether contentious or not  science affects our lives every day so  it's important we're all involved in  deciding how it's done  if you have any questions about gain of  function research or the pandemic in  general please leave them in the  comments and don't forget to hit  subscribe so you don't miss any of our  updates gracias

